.agents/workflows/architecture.js:1314-1320:
```
// Fix round: when refutation/approved fixes are passed, the author FIXES the existing skeleton rather than writing fresh.
// The REFUTE findings driving a fix round arrive as a file path, never as content, so one copy
// exists and nothing can be altered on the way here. The agent opens the file itself.
const refutationPath = input && input.refutationPath
if (refutationPath !== undefined && (typeof refutationPath !== 'string' || !refutationPath.trim())) {
  throw new Error('architecture: refutationPath must be a nonempty file path when provided')
}
```
.agents/workflows/rule-phase.js:1264-1270 and .agents/workflows/tdd.js:1321-1327 contain the byte-for-byte same 5-line comment+guard, differing only in the substituted stage name ("architecture"/"rule-phase"/"tdd") and one noun ("skeleton"/"rules"/"tests") in the leading comment — confirmed by direct sed -n extraction and diff of all three ranges. This is exactly the class of duplication `dry-shared-module-authority`/`copied-module-convention` was built to eliminate (5 other cross-cutting behaviors were already extracted into named copied blocks: workflow-input, required-agent-runtime, stage-result-contracts, selected-rule-warnings, prior-art-ledger), yet this sixth piece of shared behavior was left as silent, unmarked copies. It is not listed in the contract's Reuse ledger (9 rows, none naming this validation). The codebase already contains two DIFFERENT generalized ways to do the identical kind of check (design.js:48-52 loops over [['factsPath', factsPath], ['ledgerPath', ledgerPath]]; refute.js:52-57 loops over Object.entries(stageOutputPaths)), proving the same duplication class was recognized and generalized twice, yet a third, narrower shape was still hand-copied into three more files instead of reusing either existing generalized approach or the copied-module mechanism.
